>) this is given by an\nelement <MATH_0018>. We omit the verification\nthat these <MATH_0019>'s give a <MATH_0020>-cocycle, i.e., give\nan element <MATH_0021>\nwith <MATH_0022>. Thus its class is an element in\n<MATH_0023> and by\nTheorem <REF_0002>\nit maps to a cohomology class in <MATH_0024>.\n\n<STRUCT_0006><STRUCT_0007>\nSecond construction: Using torsors. Consider the presheaf\n<MATH_0025>\non <MATH_0026>.\nWe may view this as a subpresheaf of\n<MATH_0027> (internal hom\nsheaf, see\nModules on Sites, Section <REF_0003>).\nSince the conditions defining this subpresheaf are local, we see that it is\na sheaf.\nBy (<REF_0004>) this sheaf has a free action of\nthe sheaf <MATH_0028>. Hence the only thing we have to check is that\nit locally has sections. This is true because of the existence of the\ntrivializing cover <MATH_0029>. Hence <MATH_0030>\nis a <MATH_0031>-torsor and by\nCohomology on Sites, Lemma <REF_0005>\nwe obtain a corresponding element of <MATH_0032>.\n\n<STRUCT_0008><STRUCT_0009>\nOk, now we have to still show the following\n<STRUCT_0010>\n<STRUCT_0011> The two constructions give the same cohomology class.\n<STRUCT_0012> Isomorphic pairs give rise to the same cohomology class.\n<STRUCT_0013> The cohomology class of\n<MATH_0033>\nis the sum of the cohomology classes of\n<MATH_0034> and <MATH_0035>.\n<STRUCT_0014> If the cohomology class is trivial, then the pair is trivial.\n<STRUCT_0015> Any element of <MATH_0036> is the\ncohomology class of a pair.\n<STRUCT_0016>\nWe omit the proof of (1). Part (2) is clear from the second construction,\nsince isomorphic torsors give the same cohomology classes.\nPart (3) is clear from the first construction, since the resulting\n<GROUPOPEN_0002><ACCENT_0002><GROUPCLOSE_0002>ech classes add up. Part (4) is clear from the second construction\nsince a torsor is trivial if and only if it has a global section, see\nCohomology on Sites, Lemma <REF_0006>.\n\n<STRUCT_0017><STRUCT_0018>\nPart (5) can be seen as follows (a